4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4],[3,3,4,8,8,4,0,0,4,0,0,4,0,0,4,0,0,4,0,0,4,0,0,4,0,0,4,0,0],[3,3,4,8,8,4,0,0,4,0,0,4,0,0,4,0,0,4,0,0,4,0,0,4,0,0,4,0,0]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## What the grids are made of\n\nEvery input is a **lattice**. Full rows and columns of one colour (3 in pair 1, 8 in pairs 2 and 3, 4 in the test) cut the grid into equal square cells: 3×3, 4×4 or 2×2. Each cell is filled with a single colour, so the whole puzzle can be read on a small \"cell grid\" with one value per cell. Most cells are empty (0).\n\nOn that cell grid, each input has two kinds of content:\n\n- **One multi-cell pattern (the template).** It has one centre cell, and the other colours are arranged around it.\n  - Pair 1: centre 4, with 6s directly above, below, left and right (a plus shape).\n  - Pair 2: centre 6, with 3s in a plus shape.\n  - Pair 3: centre 2, with a full 3×3 ring of 4s.\n  - Test: centre 6, with 3s directly above, below, left and right, and 8s on the diagonals.\n- **Lone cells that have the template's centre colour**, sitting by themselves.\n  - Pair 1: lone 4s at cell (0,6) and cell (5,3).\n  - Pair 2: a lone 6 at cell (4,3).\n  - Pair 3: lone 2s at cell (0,6) and cell (1,1).\n  - Test: lone 6s at cell (1,7), cell (7,5) and cell (8,0).\n\n## What the output does\n\nIn every pair, each lone centre-coloured cell gets the template's surroundings copied around it, at the same relative offsets. Where the copy would fall outside the grid, it is simply cut off. The demonstrations show this clipping:\n\n- Pair 1: the 4 in the top-right corner gains only a 6 to its left and a 6 below it.\n- Pair 2: the 6 on the bottom row gains 3s to the left, the right and above, but nothing below.\n- Pair 3: the 2 at the top edge gets only the lower part of its ring.\n\nThe fact sheet supports this. The only changes are 0→new colour, and nothing that was already in the input is altered. In pair 3, the 2 at cell (1,1) gets its complete 3×3 ring of 4s, which makes the large 4-region in the top-left of the output. That matches the 99 changed cells.\n\n## How the reference program implements this\n\nReading the reference program, it follows this rule directly:\n\n1. **Find the lattice.** It looks for full single-colour rows and columns by geometry, not by \"least common colour\". This matters for the test, where the line colour 4 is the most frequent colour in the grid. It then builds the cell grid, and the most common cell value counts as \"empty\".\n2. **Split the content.** It takes the 8-connected components of non-empty cells. The largest one with more than one cell is the template. One-cell components are the singles.\n3. **Find the template's centre.** It is the colour that appears exactly once in the template and also appears among the singles.\n4. **Stamp the template.** For each single of the centre colour, it copies every template cell at its offset from the centre. It writes only into cells that are empty and inside the grid, and it paints the whole lattice cell in the full-resolution output.\n\nThe logic agrees with all three demonstrations as analysed above, and it covers the test case's different cell size (2×2), grid size (29×29), and line colour. In the test, each of the three lone 6s receives 3s above, below, left and right and 8s on the diagonals. Clipping applies wherever the stamp runs off the grid; for example, the 6 at cell (8,0) in the left column loses its left side.\n\n**Emit checkpoints:** one for the starting canvas, one after each stamp, and one for the final result.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    import collections\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    # Lattice lines: full rows/columns of one colour (may be the modal colour).\n    drows = [y for y in range(H) if (g[y] == g[y, 0]).all()]\n    dcols = [x for x in range(W) if (g[:, x] == g[0, x]).all()]\n\n    def spans(divs, n):\n        out, s = [], 0\n        for v in divs + [n]:\n            if v > s:\n                out.append((s, v - 1))\n            s = v + 1\n        return out\n\n    rs, cs = spans(drows, H), spans(dcols, W)\n    R, C = len(rs), len(cs)\n    cell = np.array([[g[rs[i][0], cs[j][0]] for j in range(C)] for i in range(R)])\n    vals, cnts = np.unique(cell, return_counts=True)\n    empty = vals[np.argmax(cnts)]\n\n    comps = components(cell != empty, diagonal=True)\n    template = [c for c in comps if len(c) > 1]\n    singles = [c[0] for c in comps if len(c) == 1]\n    out = g.copy()\n    emit(out, \"Input: a lattice of cells holding one multi-cell template pattern plus lone cells of the template's centre colour.\")\n    if not template:\n        return out\n    tmpl = max(template, key=len)\n    tcols = collections.Counter(int(cell[y, x]) for y, x in tmpl)\n    centre_col = None\n    cy = cx = 0\n    for (y, x) in tmpl:\n        if tcols[int(cell[y, x])] == 1 and any(cell[sy, sx] == cell[y, x] for sy, sx in singles):\n            centre_col, cy, cx = cell[y, x], y, x\n            break\n    if centre_col is None:\n        return out\n\n    def paint(i, j, col):\n        y0, y1 = rs[i]\n        x0, x1 = cs[j]\n        out[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1] = col\n\n    for (sy, sx) in singles:\n        if cell[sy, sx] != centre_col:\n            continue\n        for (ty, tx) in tmpl:\n            ny, nx = sy + ty - cy, sx + tx - cx\n            if 0 <= ny < R and 0 <= nx < C and cell[ny, nx] == empty:\n                cell[ny, nx] = cell[ty, tx]\n                paint(ny, nx, cell[ty, tx])\n        emit(out, f\"Stamped the template's surroundings around the lone centre cell at lattice cell ({sy},{sx}), clipped at the grid edge.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final: every lone centre-coloured cell now carries the full template pattern.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Split the content.** It takes the 8-connected components of non-empty cells."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"This matters for the test, where the line colour 4 is the most frequent colour in the grid."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It then builds the cell grid, and the most common cell value counts as \"empty\"."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The largest one with more than one cell is the template."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **One multi-cell pattern (the template).** It has one centre cell, and the other colours are arranged around it."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Lone cells that have the template's centre colour**, sitting by themselves."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"In every pair, each lone centre-coloured cell gets the template's surroundings copied around it, at the same relative offsets."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Where the copy would fall outside the grid, it is simply cut off."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It then builds the cell grid, and the most common cell value counts as \"empty\"."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":true,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"fe2863da1996b6856ff4b2984d4cfcf054318cbff4b29147879479830fcd1dd1","task_sha256":"7f4df8d798541a6b93cf8e10645ae476caa7c8f1be144049d800ec84959de1d8","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
